Choose a suitable game technology based on the target environment.

For a browser-based 2D game, use a structured HTML5 game framework or a carefully organized Canvas-based implementation.

For a more elaborate 2D or 3D experience, use an appropriate dedicated game engine.

Separate gameplay logic from presentation and interface code.

Recommended logical modules:

- GameManager
- LevelManager
- InputManager
- DragDropController
- IngredientManager
- CookingManager
- RecipeManager
- OrderManager
- CustomerManager
- RestaurantManager
- EconomyManager
- ProgressionManager
- SaveManager
- AudioManager
- UIManager
- AccessibilityManager

Use reusable data-driven definitions for ingredients, recipes, customers, levels, upgrades, and rewards.

Avoid duplicating logic for every food item.

Use a central game state with explicit states such as:

- MainMenu
- Tutorial
- Playing
- Paused
- LevelComplete
- LevelFailed
- Loading

Prevent impossible state transitions and duplicate actions.

If the implementation uses asynchronous loading or timers, cancel or clean them up when the relevant screen or level ends.

## 25. PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION

The game should run smoothly on typical modern phones and desktop computers.

Optimize image sizes, texture usage, animation counts, and background effects.

Use asset compression and appropriately sized textures.

Avoid creating unnecessary objects every frame.

Reuse frequently spawned effects where practical.

Keep input responsive even when several customers and food animations are active.

Provide lower graphics settings for less powerful devices.

Do not sacrifice core gameplay responsiveness for decorative effects.

For browser implementations, prevent unwanted page scrolling while interacting with the game surface and restore normal page behavior outside the active game area.

## 26. ERROR HANDLING AND GAMEPLAY EDGE CASES

Test the following scenarios carefully:

- The player drops an ingredient outside a valid station.
- The player releases a drag outside the game area.
- The player switches tabs while dragging.
- The device rotates during a level.
- The browser window is resized.
- A cooking timer finishes while the player is interacting with another item.
- An order expires while the player is assembling a dish.
- The player attempts to serve an incorrect meal.
- The player runs out of coins.
- The player attempts to purchase the same upgrade twice.
- The player pauses during cooking.
- The player restarts a level.
- The save data is missing or corrupted.
- Multiple customers request the same dish.
- The player rapidly clicks or taps the same control.
- A touch gesture is interrupted by the operating system.

All edge cases must produce predictable results.

Never allow a player to get permanently stuck because an ingredient disappeared, a timer continued incorrectly, or a drag interaction failed.

